cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Exact-Relation Coding: A Preregistered Test of Whether Discovered Fixed-Width
  GF(2)/Affine Structure Yields a Composed Lossless-Compression Advantage
message: >-
  If you use this software or dataset, please cite it using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Xeloraa"   # PLACEHOLDER — see note above
repository-code: "https://github.com/Xeloraa/deductive-coding"
url: "https://github.com/Xeloraa/deductive-coding/releases/tag/v1.1-final"
version: "v1.1-final"
date-released: "2026-08-30"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - lossless compression
  - algebraic redundancy
  - GF(2) linear codes
  - functional dependencies
  - preregistration
  - negative result
  - reproducibility
abstract: >-
  A preregistered empirical study of whether automatically discovered exact
  fixed-width GF(2)/affine relations provide a composed lossless-compression
  advantage. The pre-pass is a fixed-width GF(2) column-basis codec with a
  bit-exact accounting ledger, a never-worse passthrough fallback, an
  independent shared-nothing verifier, and a composed-gain metric with a
  git-locked per-file success threshold fixed before any natural-corpus
  experiment. On data engineered to contain an exact GF(2) linear code the
  pre-pass removes about half of the size the six stock baselines achieve
  (1 MiB code: G_pct = +49.95 %), the gain scales with input size, and two
  context-mixing compressors (paq8l, paq8px v216) do not close it. On the
  natural data actually tested — 8 of 12 whole Silesia members, all 12 members
  plus enwik8 plus six SDRBench float32 fields plus a UCI telemetry file at
  256 KiB, and a bounded bit-phase-offset detector extension — no non-prior-art
  file produces a composed gain meeting the threshold; the largest natural
  G_pct is +0.09 % (on a passthrough container, i.e. header perturbation, not
  deduction). Four whole-file runs (the largest Silesia members and whole
  enwik8 / SDRBench / UCI) exceeded the 8 GiB development machine and were not
  executed, so by the preregistration the complete-corpus hypothesis outcome is
  INCONCLUSIVE; within the achieved coverage the result is a clean layered
  negative (exact structure is often present and discoverable, and on no tested
  file does it reduce the representation or survive composition). The mechanism
  is not novel.
